## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design per-tenant QoS with dynamic config in a multi-DC setup.

## Key Concepts
- Traefik RateLimit middleware, Redis store, per-tenant quotas, Docker Config
- TLS termination, hot-reload without restart, cross-DC coordination
- Request tagging with X-Tenant and validation of isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo middleware sketch: extract tenant and enforce quota from Redis
async function enforceTenantRate(req, res, next){
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
  const quota = await redisGetQuota(tenant);
  if (quota.allow(req)) return next();
  res.status(429).send('Too Many Requests');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you audit cross-DC quota drift and recover from Redis replication lag?
- How would you test hot-reload behavior under config churn?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design distributed feature-flag propagation with per-tenant isolation across data centers. It covers Docker Config for tenant flags, per-tenant in-process caching with TTL, Redis Pub/Sub for invalidation and cross-DC propagation, and edge security with TLS termination at Traefik.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config for tenant-specific flag storage
- Per-tenant in-process cache with configurable TTL
- Redis Pub/Sub for cache invalidation and propagation
- Lightweight policy/flag service coordinating across DCs
- TLS termination at Traefik edge proxy

## Code Example
```javascript
class FlagCache {
  constructor(redis, ttlSeconds = 120) {
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.ttlSeconds = ttlSeconds;
    this.redis = redis;

    redis.subscribe('tenant-flags', (err, count) => {
      if (err) console.error('Subscribe error:', err);
    });

    redis.on('message', (channel, message) => {
      if (channel === 'tenant-flags') {
        const { tenantId } = JSON.parse(message);
        this.cache.delete(tenantId);
      }
    });
  }

  async getFlag(tenantId, feature) {
    const key = `${tenantId}:${feature}`;
    const entry = this.cache.get(key);

    if (entry && Date.now() < entry.expiresAt) {
      return entry.value;
    }

    const value = await this.redis.hget(`flags:${tenantId}`, feature);
    this.cache.set(key, { value: JSON.parse(value), expiresAt: Date.now() + this.ttlSeconds * 1000 });
    return JSON.parse(value);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical ability to extend a docker-dca setup with a compliant, low-latency data-redaction path.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config-driven per-tenant policies
- Sidecar pattern and in-memory caching
- Streaming JSON redaction without full buffering
- Nginx-edge integration and TLS context

## Code Example
```go
package main
import (
  "encoding/json"
  "fmt"
)
func redact(m map[string]interface{}, fields map[string]bool){
  for k,v := range m {
    if fields[k] { m[k] = "***REDACTED***"; continue }
    if mm, ok := v.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(mm, fields) }
    if arr, ok := v.([]interface{}); ok {
      for _, it := range arr { if sub, ok := it.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(sub, fields) } }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test nested JSON redaction with large bodies?
- How would you monitor redaction performance without affecting latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of docker-dca basics, per-tenant gating, and config management in real deployments across DCs.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config for feature flags
- Hot reload of config without restart
- Tenant-scoped feature gating via header X-Tenant-ID
- TLS termination with Nginx and cross-DC routing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal per-tenant feature gating middleware
const fs = require('fs');
let features = {};
function load(){
  try{ features = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('/config/flags.json','utf8')); }catch(e){ features = {}; }
}
load();
setInterval(load, 30000);
module.exports = function(req,res,next){
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  if (tenant && features[tenant] && features[tenant].includes('payments')) return next();
  res.status(403).send('Feature disabled');
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate config schema and handle partial reloads?
- How would you extend to multiple features and tenants with minimal latency impact?
